WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:53.540
If I ask you to think of 
a country where more than 80% of

00:00:53.540 --> 00:01:13.170
the inhabitants live in state-built homes,
many will surely think of a socialist republic

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.990
or maybe a former Soviet bloc country, right?

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:16.990
But...

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.950
What if I told you that this figure corresponds
to one of the countries with the freest and

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.780
most deregulated economies?

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.850
Possibly the most capitalist country in the
world.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:26.850
Surprised?

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:31.939
Well, Singapore is an unusual case, an exception
in terms of housing models.

00:01:31.939 --> 00:01:38.380
This Southeast asian city-state, famous for
its towering skyscrapers, impossible architecture

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.409
and for being one of the largest financial
centers in the world, has developed a public

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:46.560
housing system that is globally unique.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:54.509
Some of you may be thinking, but Simon, you
already told us, on this very channel, that

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:58.100
Vienna also has an extensive public housing
system!

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.189
Well yes, you're right.

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:06.469
However, we’re talking about two very different
models.

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:12.210
Completely different.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:17.250
The great difference between Vienna and Singapore’s
model lies particularly in the percentage

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:22.280
of property owners.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.580
While the Viennese model is oriented towards
rentals, three-quarters of the population

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.780
rents city council-owned homes, the Singapore
model is just the opposite.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.400
More than 90% of Singapore’s citizens own
their own homes.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:43.790
Of course, 80% own homes promoted, built and
granted by the state.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:49.620
Yes, we’re talking ownership-oriented public
housing.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.860
Ownership with a few catches, if we’re being
completely honest.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:53.860
But...

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:54.860
We'll talk about that later.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.890
First, we need to ask ourselves some questions:

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.540
Why does Singapore apparently reject the free
market real-estate model?

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.940
How on earth can they develop a property-oriented
public housing system?

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.209
How has this model affected private real estate
developments?

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:10.170
And...Is the Singapore model replicable in
other countries?

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.489
Well, let's get to the bottom of all these
questions.

00:03:13.489 --> 00:03:16.010
Listen up.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:24.830
(SINGAPORE, LITTLE LAND AND A HUGE POPULATION)

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.280
Dear friends of VisualPolitik.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.349
First, we need to familiarise ourselves with
the context.

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:33.890
Check out this map.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.560
(MAP)

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.680
Singapore is a city-state with just over 700
km2 but with more than five and a half million

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:42.680
inhabitants.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.110
This has forced more than 70% of the land
to be built on.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.040
Those are the red areas of the map.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:55.710
The remaining 30% includes mostly protected
areas or land where one simply can’t build.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:59.230
Basically, the city is overflowing.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.260
Faced with such a scenario, it wouldn’t
be unreasonable to think that accessing a

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.740
home in Singapore would be a headache, would
it?

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.950
After all, that’s the case in similar places
such as Hong Kong.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.209
Well, strangely enough...

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:11.209
It isn’t.

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:13.969
The reason has a lot to do with Singapore’s
unique urban development model.

00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:23.470
Check this out, in 1960 the Singapore government
created the Housing and Development Board,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:28.080
a public housing company that’s responsible
for regulating and managing the land, as well

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.830
as building and developing real estate projects.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:38.900
And that’s because since 1967, most of the
land in Singapore has been publicly owned.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:44.211
In that year, 1967, the so-called Land Acquisition
Act was approved, which enabled the government

00:04:44.211 --> 00:04:46.600
to take back ownership of all the land.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.220
That means, the land marketed for real estate
development is made available under the Housing

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:51.560
Board’s criteria.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:56.500
That is, there isn’t a price system in land
commercialization.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.440
Let me explain it in another way.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.449
This public body determines how many homes
it will build; and at what prices and under

00:05:04.449 --> 00:05:12.770
what conditions they will be marketed – evidently
always below the potential market price.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.710
On the other hand, it’s this same organization,
this Housing and Development Board, which

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:23.650
draws land for auction so private developers
can build completely private real estate projects.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:32.639
Of course, in every case, the land continues
to be publicly owned, that is, the homeowners

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:36.390
cannot do whatever they want with it – for
example they can’t sell their homes to build

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:37.390
a hotel.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.690
And the property is more a concession than
a property.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:41.960
Allow me to explain myself.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:46.190
When a citizen of Singapore – and I emphasize
this because foreigners can’t buy new public

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:50.620
housing – gets one of these homes, they
aren’t really acquiring the land’s ownership

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:55.190
nor the house itself, but only the right to
stay in it, a kind of lease that lasts for

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.190
99 years.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:58.449
After that period, the house returns to the
state.

00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:01.340
This is how the Prime Minister of Singapore
explains it:

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:05.800
(AUDIO: “After 99 years, the flat comes
back to the state, the Government redevelops

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.720
the land, and builds new flats for future
generations.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:14.069
This is the only way to recycle the land,
to ensure all our descendants can buy new

00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:15.069
flats of their own.

00:06:15.069 --> 00:06:18.669
If instead the Government sold you the flat
on freehold … Sooner or later we would run

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:20.190
out of land to build new flats.”

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.210
Lee Hsien Loong, Prime Minister of Singapore.)

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:29.530
And, you have to be careful, because in private
developments this period may be even shorter.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:37.970
Of course, during the lease period “the
owners” can do whatever they deem appropriate

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:38.970
with the house.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.669
If they don't want to live in it, they can
sell it or rent it.

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:50.620
In fact, it isn’t uncommon, if things are
going well, for those who own older homes

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:54.880
to sell or rent the “public” homes, which
tend to be very austere – especially the

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.230
old ones – in order to move into private
developments, which in Singapore’s case

00:06:58.230 --> 00:06:59.530
are far more luxurious.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:08.919
But the peculiarities of this curious system
don’t end there.

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:12.990
Since the body that builds and sells most
homes is public, the government uses it as

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:14.800
a social policy instrument.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.379
Or rather, an instrument of social engineering.

00:07:17.379 --> 00:07:21.650
For example, to avoid the formation of ghettos
given that Singapore’s population has a

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:26.750
certain racial diversity, a building or development’s
dwellings are allocated in such a way that

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:31.930
the proportion of owners has an ethnic distribution
that reflects the distribution in the whole

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.410
country.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:40.860
The population distribution by ethnic group
in Singapore looks something like this:

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.680
In each block of homes the owners are distributed
more or less in the same way.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:49.379
If a block or a project has, for example,
100 homes, then we’ll find 74 Chinese owners,

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:52.810
13 Malaysian owners, 9 Indian owners, and
so on.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.259
However, that’s not a bad thing.

00:07:56.259 --> 00:08:00.199
Perhaps the worst aspect of this model is
that the government uses the granting of homes

00:08:00.199 --> 00:08:02.520
to promote certain life models.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.670
For example, traditional couples don’t have
any problems applying for a government property,

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:08.580
but single people or same-sex couples…

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:13.699
Well, let's just say it’s much more complicated
for them.

00:08:13.699 --> 00:08:14.860
Surprised?

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:20.789
Isn’t it strange to find such interventionism
in a supposedly capitalist place?

00:08:20.789 --> 00:08:25.081
Well, it shouldn’t be surprising, we already
told you that Singapore’s political, economic

00:08:25.081 --> 00:08:30.120
and social model is a kind of mix between
free-market capitalist policies and very,

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:35.349
very paternalistic policies where the state
actively promotes certain ways of life and

00:08:35.349 --> 00:08:40.740
punishes others.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:45.030
In this case, in the area of housing, not
only in housing allocation, but also, for

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:49.670
example, mortgage approvals, conditions are
very advantageous for people who meet the

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:50.880
state-imposed criteria.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:52.810
If you don't, well... bad luck.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:55.890
Do you want to live alone?

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:56.890
Sorry.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:00.330
Do you want to live with a friend?

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:01.330
Too bad.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:03.120
Do you want to live with your same-sex partner?

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.870
Well... you know... we wish we could help
but...

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:16.180
Now, beyond these rather questionable practices,
the truth is that this system has been quite

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.180
successful.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.440
Public housing is much more affordable in
Singapore than in other cities with comparable

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.520
economic success, such as London, Sydney or
Hong Kong.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:28.420
On average, new buyers spend less than a quarter
of their income on their mortgage.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:29.960
But... just a second.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:37.230
All this isn’t free.

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:44.110
(THE ECONOMIC INVOICE DILEMMA)

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:48.660
If the government of Singapore is known for
one thing, it’s for keeping taxes and public

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:50.150
spending at bay.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:53.330
However, things are different with this whole
housing issue.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:54.890
The system is clearly lacking:

00:09:54.890 --> 00:10:02.630
(AUDIO: HDB reports $1.47 billion loss in
latest financial year as fewer flats sold

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:03.720
- Channel New Asia)

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:07.720
1.5 billion dollars, that’s the difference
between what urbanization and construction

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:11.750
work costs the state and the income it receives
from selling the homes.

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:15.470
That’s 1.5 billion dollars that is financed
from the public budget.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:19.210
The whole housing scheme is basically financed
with taxes.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:23.910
Regardless of land ownership, this is the
annual allocation that the Singapore government

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:25.290
devotes to its housing policy.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.870
A perfectly acceptable amount for an economy
as big as Singapore’s.

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:32.950
Now, does this mean that the governments of
other countries should take back ownership

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:37.630
of city lands and oversee housing development,
just like the Singapore government does?

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:41.440
Not at all.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.890
Singapore’s case is very particular.

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:51.390
We’re talking about a city-state with huge
economic success, a state which is managed

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:55.510
almost like a company and where land limitations
are more than evident.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:57.880
The differences with other countries are quite
obvious.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:01.890
Nor is it clear that if it was operating as
a free market – a truly free one – the

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.700
results would be much different.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:08.010
Not to mention the enormous power that such
systems grant politicians to design societies

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:12.560
at will.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:18.770
But what Singapore has really achieved with
this model – and I think it’s undeniable

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:23.130
– has been to develop an urban planning
and real estate price scheme that favors the

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:26.580
development of many other activities with
greater added value.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:30.750
For example, given that housing prices are
capped, Singaporean citizens have a greater

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:36.570
capacity to save and invest in, for example,
new businesses.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:41.340
Anyway, this is Singapore’s unique model,
yet another model up for debate.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.690
So, now it’s your turn.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.120
What do you think of the Singaporean housing
model?

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:46.120
Leave your answer in the comments.

